In this section, we will learn about compact sets in arbitrary topological
spaces.

#label(defin:compact-net)
useenv defin [Compact sets] {
    Let $X$ be a topological space. Then a set $X$ is said to be {\itshape
    compact}\index{set!compact} if for every collection $\Us$ of open sets which
    cover $X$, we can find a finite subcollection $\Fs\subset\Us$ such that
    $\bigcup\Fs\supset X$. The collection of type $\Us$ described in above is
    called an {\itshape open cover of $X$ }\index{open cover}. A subset
    $K\subset X$ is said to be compact if $K$ is compact within the subspace
    topology. A set is said to be {\itshape precompact}\index{set!precompact} if
    the closure of which is compact.
}

We can show that the space is compact by using nets. Here is the theorem about.
#label(thm:compact-net)
useenv thm {
    Let $X$ be a topological space. Then $X$ is compact if and only if every
    universal net in $X$ converges.
    \proof
    Suppose that there is a universal net $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ which
    converges nowhere in $X$. For each $x\in X$ take an open set $U_x$ which
    the net is not eventually in. Note that clearly $\{U_x\}_{x\in X}$ is an
    open cover of $X$. If $X$ were compact, there are finitely many points
    $x_1,...,x_n$ such that
    useenv equation { \label{eq:thm:compact-net:1}
        U_{x_1}\cup U_{x_2}\cup...\cup U_{x_n}\supset X.
    }
    However as $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ is a universal net, it is eventually
    in $X-!U_x$ for each $x\in X$ by \Cref{prop:equiv-universal-net}. This and
    \cref{eq:thm:compact-net:1} gives
    $$
        (x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A} \text{ is eventually in } (X-!U_{x_1})\cap
        (X-!U_{x_2})\cap...\cap(X-!U_{x_n}) = \emptyset,
    $$
    which is a contradiction. Such contradiction occurs as we assumed that $X$
    is compact. Hence $X$ cannot be compact.

    Conversely, suppose that $X$ is not compact. Then there is an open cover
    $\Us$ which does not have a finite subset of $\Us$ that covers $X$. Put
    $$
        \Vs \defeq \{X-!E : E\in\Us\}.
    $$
    Observe that $\Vs$ satisfies FIP. Take a universal net
    $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ which is eventually in every set in $\Vs$ by
    \Cref{prop:generate-net-fip} and \Cref{prop:existence-universal-net}. Since
    $\Us$ is an open cover of $X$, there is no $x\in X$ such that
    $x_\alpha -> x$. Indeed, if $x\in X$ then there is $U\in\Us$ that contains
    $x$. Since $X-!U\in \Vs$, the net is eventually in $X-!U$ and this implies
    that the net does not converge to $x$. \proved
}

#label(coro:compact-net)
useenv coro {
    Let $X$ be a topological space. Then nonempty subset $K\subset X$ is compact
    if and only if every universal net in $X$ eventually in $K$ converges to
    some point in $K$. Notice that the convergent point can differ.
    \proof
    Suppose that $K\subset X$ is compact, and fix a universal net
    $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ that is eventually in $K$. Then the subnet
    $(y_\beta)_{\beta\in B}$ restricted to indices where $x_\alpha\in K$ is a
    universal net in $K$ by \Cref{exec:restricted-universal}. Being compact,
    $y_\beta -> x\in K$ by \Cref{thm:compact-net}. Now fix an open set $N$ which
    contains $x$. Since $N\cap K$ is open in $K$ and $x\in N\cap K$ we have
    $(y_\beta)_{\beta\in B}$ is eventually in $N\cap K$ because it converges to
    $x$ in $K$.

    In order to prove that $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ is eventually in $N$,
    assume that it is not. Then by \Cref{prop:equiv-universal-net}, it is
    eventually in $X-!N$, thus $(y_\beta)_{\beta\in B}$ is eventually in
    $(X-!N)\cap K$. Since it is also eventually in $N\cap K$, we must obtain
    $$
        (y_\beta)_{\beta\in B} \text{ is eventually in } (N\cap K)\cap[(X-!N)\cap
        K] = \emptyset,
    $$
    which is a contradiction. Therefore, $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ is
    eventually in $N$, and thus we prove that $x_\alpha -> x$ since $N$ is
    arbitrary.

    To prove the sufficiency, claim that if $(y_\beta)_{\beta\in B}$ is any
    universal net in $K$, then there exists a universal net
    $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ in $X$ eventually in $K$ whose subnet corresponds
    to it. By our assumption, $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ converges to some
    $x\in K$. By the definition of the subspace topology, it is easy to verify
    that $y_\beta -> x$. Therefore, $K$ is compact because of
    \Cref{thm:compact-net}.
    \proved
}

#label(exec:restricted-universal)
useenv exec* {
    Prove that the restricted subnet in $K$ in \Cref{coro:compact-net} is really
    a universal net in $K$.
}

Below propositions can be proved without using any net theory. In here, however,
we will see how \Cref{thm:compact-net} (and \Cref{coro:compact-net}) can be used
to prove several compactness properties. One may solve
\Cref{exec:properties-compact}.

#label(prop:properties-compact)
useenv prop {
    Let $X$ be a topological space and $K\subset X$ be a nonempty set.
    #enum ((\alph*)) {
        \item If $X$ is Hausdorff and $K$ is compact, then $K$ is closed.
        \item If $X$ is compact and $K$ is closed, then $K$ is compact.
        \item Let $Y$ be another topological space and $f:X->Y$ a continuous map,
        then $f[K]$ is compact in $Y$ whenever $K$ is compact in $X$.
    }
    \proof
    (a) Fix $x\in\overline{K}$ and take a subnet $(y_\beta)_{\beta\in B}$ in $X$
    such that it is in $K$ and $y_\beta -> x$ by \Cref{thm:net-closure-conti}.
    Then take a universal subnet $(z_\gamma)_{\gamma\in C}$ by
    \Cref{prop:existence-universal-net}. Since $y_\beta -> x$ and
    $(z_\gamma)_{\gamma\in C}$ is a subnet, we have $z_\gamma -> x$. On the
    other hand, since $K$ is compact, and $(z_\gamma)_{\gamma\in C}$ is
    eventually in $K$, by \Cref{coro:compact-net}, there exists $y\in K$ such
    that $z_\gamma -> y$. Since $X$ is Hausdorff, $x$ and $y$ must be equal
    because of \Cref{thm:net-hausdorff}, proving that $x=y\in K$. Since $x$ is
    arbitrary element of $\overline{K}$, we proved that $K$ is closed.

    (b) Fix a universal net $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ in $X$ which is
    eventually in $K$. Being compact, $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ converges to
    some point $x\in X$. By \Cref{thm:net-closure-conti}, $x\in\overline{K}=K$
    . Hence, $K$ is compact because of \Cref{coro:compact-net} as
    $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ is arbitrary.

    (c) Fix a universal net $(z_\gamma)_{\gamma\in C}$ in $f[K]$. Then take a
    universal net $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ in $K$ such that $f_*(x_\alpha)$ is
    a subnet of $(z_\gamma)_{\gamma\in C}$. Being compact, we can take a point
    $x\in K$ such that $x_\alpha -> x$ by \Cref{thm:compact-net}. Since $f$ is
    continuous, $f_*(x_\alpha) -> f(x)$ because of \Cref{thm:net-closure-conti}.
    Observe that since both are universal nets and one is a subnet of another,
    they converge to the same points. Therefore, $z_\gamma -> f(x)$, and we
    proved that $f[K]$ is compact by \Cref{thm:compact-net} as
    $(z_\gamma)_{\gamma\in C}$ is arbitrary. \proved
}

#label(exec:properties-compact)
useenv exec {
    Prove \Cref{prop:properties-compact} without using any net theories.
}

We studied what is a compact set, how this is related to filter theory, and
studied some properties about compact sets.
Checking whether a set is compact is very difficult.
On the ordered space, under some conditions, every closed interval is compact.

#label(thm:closed-interval-compact)
useenv thm {
    Let $X$ be an order space which has a least upper bound property.
    Then every closed interval is compact.
    \proof
    Fix any closed interval $[a,b]$, and an ultrafilter $\Uc$ in $X$ which
    contains $[a,b]$. Consider
    $$
        A \defeq \{ x\in [a,b] : [x,b] \in \Uc\}.
    $$
    Since $[a,b]\in\Uc$, the set $A$ is not empty, and clearly $A$ has an upper
    bound, which is $b$. Thus the supremum of $A$ exists, let us call it
    $\alpha$. Claim that $\Uc->\alpha$.
    Since filters are closed under finite intersection and contains all
    supersets of their elements, it suffices to consider open rays in $[a,b]$
    which contain $\alpha$.

    First, consider $(x,b]$ where $x\in [a,b)$ and that $\alpha\in(x,b]$.
    Since $\alpha$ is the supremum and $x < \alpha$, there exists $\beta\in
    (x,\alpha]\cap A$. That means, $[\beta,b]\in\Uc$.
    Since $(x,b] \supset [\beta,b]$, we thus obtain $(x,b]\in\Uc$.

    Now consider $[a,x)$ where $x\in (a,b]$ and that $\alpha\in[a,x)$.
    If $[a,x)$ does not belong to $\Uc$, \Cref{prop:equiv-ultrafilters}
    and $[a,b]\in\Uc$ imply
    $$
        [x,b] = (X-![a,x)) \cap [a,b] \in \Uc,
    $$
    thus $x\in A$. However we take $\alpha$ the supremum of $A$ and we shows
    that $x$ belongs to $A$ although $x > \alpha$, which is a contradiction
    indeed. Therefore, $[a,x)\in\Uc$ should be true, and we proved that
    $\Uc->\alpha$.

    Since $\Uc$ be any ultrafilter in $X$ containing $[a,b]$,
    \Cref{coro:compact-filter} gives that $[a,b]$ is compact. \proved
}

Compact spaces are also related to several separation axioms.
One of the most important theorem is the following.

#label(thm:compact-normal)
useenv thm {
    Every compact Hausdorff space is normal.
    \proof
    Let $X$ be any compact Hausdorff space.
    Fix any two disjoint closed subsets $A$ and $B$ of $X$.
    Being compact, both $A$ and $B$ are compact by
    \Cref{prop:properties-compact}.
    Fix $a\in A$. Since $X$ is Hausdorff, for each $b\in B$, we can take a
    disjoint open sets $U_{ab}\in\Nc_a$ and $V_{ab}\in\Nc_b$.
    Then we have an open cover $\{V_{ab}\}_{b\in B}$ of $B$, thus there exists
    finitely many points $b_1,...,b_n\in B$ such that
    $$
        B \subset V_{ab_1} \cup ... \cup V_{ab_n}
    $$
    because $B$ is compact.
    We now define
    $$
        U_a \defeq \bigcap_{j=1}^n U_{ab_j},\quad
        V_a \defeq \bigcup_{j=1}^n V_{ab_j}.
    $$
    Then $U_a$ is an open set containing $a$, $V_a \supset B$, and $U_a$ and
    $V_a$ are disjoint. Notice that such $U_a$ and $V_a$ can be obtained for
    every $a\in A$.

    Now we have an open cover $\{U_a\}_{a\in A}$ of $A$.
    Being compact, there exists a finitely many points $a_1,...,a_m\in A$ such
    that
    $$
        A \subset U_{a_1} \cup ... \cup U_{a_m}.
    $$
    Now two open sets
    $$
        U \defeq \bigcup_{j=1}^m U_{a_j},\quad
        V \defeq \bigcap_{j=1}^m V_{a_j}
    $$
    contains $A$ and $B$, respectively, and they are disjoint.
    Therefore we proved that the space $X$ is T$_4$.
    Since $X$ is Hausdorff, it is T$_1$ by \Cref{prop:hausdorff-T1}, hence we
    proved that $X$ is normal. \proved
}

On a compact space, semicontinuity gives a uniform convergence when the sequence
is increasing. Such is so called Dini's convergence theorem. Although the
original one just say about continuous function, we can generalized into
semicontinuous functions.

#label(thm:dini-theorem)
useenv thm [Dini's Convergence Theorem] {
    Let $X$ be a compact topological space, and $(f_n)_n$ be a sequence of
    real-valued LSC functions such that $f_n\uparrow f$ as $n->oo$ where $f$ is
    continuous. In other words, $f_n <= f_{n+1} <= f$ for every $n$ and $f_n ->
    f$ as $n->oo$. Then $f_n -> f$ uniformly on $X$ as $n->oo$.
    \proof
    By considering $g_n = f_n - f$, which is still LSC because of \Cref{prop:props-usc-and-lsc},
    we can assume that $f = 0$ and $f_n <= 0$ for every $n$.
    
    Fix $\epsilon>0$ and consider sets $K_n = \{f_n <= -\epsilon\}$.
    Since $f_n$ is LSC, each $K_n$ is closed by \Cref{prop:lsc-and-open},
    hence compact since $X$ is compact.
    Notice that $K_n \supset K_{n+1}$ for every $n$, and
    $$
        \bigcap_{n=1}^oo K_n = \{0 <= -\epsilon\} = \emptyset.
    $$
    Hence, by \Cref{prop:properties-compact}, there must exist a positive integer $N$
    such that $K_N$ is empty. This means that for every $n >= N$,
    $-\epsilon < f_n <= 0$ on $X$, therefore we proved that $f_n -> 0$ uniformly on $X$. \proved 
}

useenv remark {
    On the proof of \Cref{thm:dini-theorem}, we just use the upper semicontinuity of $f$.
    However, as we proved the uniform convergence, $f$ will be LSC, hence $f$ should be
    continuous.
}

We close this section by proving remarkable theorem, so called Tychonoff's theorem.
This states that product of compact spaces remains compact under the product topology.

\index{Tychonoff's Theorem}
#label(thm:tychonoff) useenv thm [Tychonoff's Theorem] {
    Let $\{X_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in A}$ be a collection of compact topological
    spaces where $A$ is an index set.
    Then the space $X = \prod_{\alpha\in A} X_\alpha$ equipped with the product
    topology is compact.
    \proof
    Let us define a projection $\pi_\alpha : X \to X_\alpha$ for each $\alpha\in
    A$, and fix an ultrafilter $\Uc$ in $X$. By \Cref{prop:pushforward-ultrafilters},
    $(\pi_\alpha)_*\Uc$ is an ultrafilter in $X_\alpha$, so we can take
    a point $x_\alpha\in X_\alpha$ such that $(\pi_\alpha)_*\Uc\to
    x_\alpha$. Such points exists because $X_\alpha$ is compact and recall
    \Cref{thm:compact-filter}. Finally, take the point $x\in X$ such that
    $\pi_\alpha(x)=x_\alpha$ for every $\alpha\in A$. Claim that $\Uc\to x$.

    To prove this, fix an open neighborhood $N$ of $x$ in $X$.
    Since
    \[
        \{ \{\pi_\alpha \in U\} : \alpha\in A,\ U\in X_\alpha\}
    \]
    forms a subbasis of $X$, there exists $\alpha_1,\cdots,\alpha_n\in A$, and
    $U_{\alpha_1},\cdots,U_{\alpha_n}$ are open sets in $X_{\alpha_j}$,
    $j=1,2,\cdots,n$, respectively, such that
    \[
        x \in 
        \{\pi_{\alpha_1} \in U_{\alpha_1}\}
        \cap\{\pi_{\alpha_2} \in U_{\alpha_2}\}
        \cap\cdots\cap\{\pi_{\alpha_n} \in U_{\alpha_n}\}
        \subset N.
    \]
    Notice that $x_{\alpha_j}\in U_{\alpha_j}$ for $j=1,2,\cdots,n$.
    This gives that $U_{\alpha_j}\in(\pi_\alpha)_*\Uc$, hence
    \[
        \{\pi_{\alpha_j}\in U_{\alpha_j}\} \in \pi_\alpha^*(\pi_\alpha)_*\Uc
        \subset\Uc.
    \]
    Thus,
    \[
        \{\pi_{\alpha_1} \in U_{\alpha_1}\}
        \cap\{\pi_{\alpha_2} \in U_{\alpha_2}\}
        \cap\cdots\cap\{\pi_{\alpha_n} \in U_{\alpha_n}\}
        \in\Uc,
    \]
    and this gives $N\in\Uc$ because $\Uc$ is an ultrafilter.
    Since $N$ is arbitrary open neighborhood of $x$, we have proved that $\Uc\to
    x$. Therefore, $X$ is compact because of \Cref{thm:compact-filter}. \proved
}

useenv remark {
    To prove \Cref{thm:tychonoff}, we need the axiom of choice. However at the
    first glance, there is no axiom of choice is used. But remember that while
    proving \Cref{prop:existence-ultrafilters} we use the axiom of choice.
    In addition, the space $X_\alpha$ might not be Hausdorff. This means that
    one much \lq\lq choose\rq\rq\ one $x_\alpha$ from several points at which the
    ultrafilter $(\pi_\alpha)_*\Uc$ converges.

    Actually, \Cref{thm:tychonoff} and the axiom of choice are equivalent.
    This means that one cannot prove \Cref{thm:tychonoff} without using the
    axiom of choice although one can use the ultrafilter lemma (see
    \Cref{remark:ultrafilter-lemma}).
    However, if all spaces $X_\alpha$ are \it{Hausdorff}, then
    \Cref{thm:tychonoff} can be proved only applying the ultrafilter lemma not
    using the axiom of choice.
}

useenv remark {
    If the index set $A$ is finite, we can prove that the product space is
    compact without using the axiom of choice.
    Notice that in this case, we cannot apply any filter theory, especially
    \Cref{thm:compact-filter}, since this uses ultrafilter lemma, and we proved
    this using the axiom of choice (see \Cref{prop:existence-ultrafilters} and
    \Cref{remark:ultrafilter-lemma}).
    One can see the proof the finite case at
    \cite{Bourbaki-topology,Munkres-topology} for instance.
}
